        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains August 19 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 2 of 4.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For August 19 in music history, a different record was number one in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes August 19.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"Alone Again (Naturally)" had the most weeks, with six, and four songs had four each: "Close to You," "How Can You Mend a Broken Heart," "Don't Go Breaking My Heart" and "I Just Want to Be Your Everything."</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week, and all three of them, "Touch Me in the Morning," "The Night Chicago Died" and "Good Times," had just that one week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} were in the final week of their runs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only August 19 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the August 19 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called August 19.</p>
        <p>This August 19 in music history page uses the weekly Hot 100 week that contains August 19 each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>Eight of the ten runs were unbroken.</p>
        <p>For those, "week of run" counts the weeks since the song first reached number one.</p>
        <p>Two runs were split by another song: "Alone Again (Naturally)" in 1972 and "I Just Want to Be Your Everything" in 1977.</p>
        <p>For those two, it counts only the weeks the song itself held number one.</p>

        <h2 id="early">August 19 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Bacharach Classic, a Bee Gees Reunion Song and a Ballad Behind a Sampling Lawsuit</h2>
        <p>August 19 in music history opened the decade with a Carpenters hit written by Burt Bacharach.</p>
        <p>Next came the Bee Gees' first American number one.</p>
        <p>Then came a six-week Gilbert O'Sullivan ballad.</p>

        <h3>1970: "(They Long to Be) Close to You" by The Carpenters</h3>
        <p>Burt Bacharach wrote the music and Hal David the lyrics.</p>
        <p>Richard Chamberlain made the first recording in 1963 as "They Long to Be Close to You."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Herb Alpert recorded it but never released his version, and he suggested the Carpenters record it.</p>
        <p>Richard Carpenter added the parentheses to the title because he thought the original was too long.</p>
        <p>A&amp;M released it on May 14, 1970, from the album Close to You, produced by Jack Daugherty.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows four weeks at number one, from July 25 to August 15, 1970.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the fourth and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/close-to-you/">Close to You</a> page.</p>
        <p>More of the duo is on the <a href="/blog/artists/carpenters-songs/">Carpenters songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1971: "How Can You Mend a Broken Heart" by Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>Barry and Robin Gibb wrote it in August 1970, after a period of separation and estrangement.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says they first offered it to Andy Williams but recorded it themselves, and Robin said it took about an hour.</p>
        <p>Robert Stigwood and the group produced it, recorded on January 28, 1971, at IBC Studios in London.</p>
        <p>Atco released it in the United States on May 28, 1971, as the lead single from the album Trafalgar.</p>
        <p>It became the group's first number one on the Billboard Hot 100.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows four weeks at number one, from August 7 to August 28, 1971.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the second of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 5 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/how-can-you-mend-a-broken-heart/">How Can You Mend a Broken Heart</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1972: "Alone Again (Naturally)" by Gilbert O'Sullivan</h3>
        <p>Gilbert O'Sullivan wrote "Alone Again (Naturally)," and Gordon Mills produced it.</p>
        <p>It was released in the United Kingdom on February 18, 1972, and in the United States in May 1972.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it sold over two million copies.</p>
        <p>The same page says the song figured in the 1991 case Grand Upright Music v. Warner Bros. Records.</p>
        <p>The case was about Biz Markie's unauthorized sample, and the ruling affected how hip-hop producers handle samples.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows six weeks at number one that were not in a row.</p>
        <p>They were July 29, August 5, August 12, August 19, September 2 and September 9, 1972.</p>
        <p>Looking Glass's "Brandy (You're a Fine Girl)" held number one for the week of August 26 in between.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the fourth of six.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">August 19 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Motown Ballad, a Fictional Chicago Shoot-Out and a Song Born on a Causeway</h2>
        <p>August 19 in music history in the middle of the decade brought a Diana Ross ballad.</p>
        <p>A British band singing about a gangland shoot-out followed.</p>
        <p>Then the Bee Gees returned to the top.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Touch Me in the Morning" by Diana Ross</h3>
        <p>Ron Miller and Michael Masser wrote "Touch Me in the Morning."</p>
        <p>Masser and Tom Baird produced it.</p>
        <p>Motown released it on May 3, 1973, as the first single from the album of the same name.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it Ross's second solo number one on the Billboard Hot 100.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of August 18, 1973.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 10 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of her career is on the <a href="/blog/artists/diana-ross-songs/">Diana Ross songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1974: "The Night Chicago Died" by Paper Lace</h3>
        <p>Peter Callander and Mitch Murray wrote and produced "The Night Chicago Died," which was released on June 15, 1974.</p>
        <p>It tells of a fictional shoot-out between Chicago police and gangsters linked to Al Capone.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the St. Valentine's Day Massacre inspired it.</p>
        <p>The writers said they had never been to Chicago and based their picture of it on gangster films.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Paper Lace sent the song to Mayor Richard Daley, who disliked it.</p>
        <p>Callander defended the song's "East Side of Chicago" setting by saying, "There's an East Side of everywhere!"</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of August 17, 1974.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 47 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Jive Talkin'" by Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>Barry, Robin and Maurice Gibb wrote "Jive Talkin'."</p>
        <p>Arif Mardin produced it at Criteria Studios in Miami in early 1975.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the working title was "Drive Talking."</p>
        <p>The beat was modeled on the sound of their car crossing the Julia Tuttle Causeway.</p>
        <p>Blue Weaver played the synthesizer bass on an ARP 2600.</p>
        <p>RSO released it in May 1975 as the lead single from the album Main Course.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, August 9 and August 16, 1975.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 12 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/jive-talkin/">Jive Talkin'</a> page, and the group has its own <a href="/blog/artists/bee-gees/">Bee Gees</a> page.</p>

        <h2 id="late">August 19 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: A Duet Offered to Dusty Springfield, an Andy Gibb Return, a Wedding Waltz and a Chic Bassline</h2>
        <p>August 19 in music history closed the decade with an Elton John duet and a second Andy Gibb run.</p>
        <p>A Commodores ballad and a Chic single that others copied followed.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Don't Go Breaking My Heart" by Elton John and Kiki Dee</h3>
        <p>Elton John and Bernie Taupin wrote it under the pseudonyms "Ann Orson" and "Carte Blanche."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says they first offered the song to Dusty Springfield but withdrew the offer.</p>
        <p>John's vocals and the musicians were recorded on March 27, 1976, at Eastern Sound in Toronto.</p>
        <p>Kiki Dee added her part at Marquee Studios in London.</p>
        <p>Gus Dudgeon produced it, and Rocket and MCA released it on June 25, 1976.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows four weeks at number one, from August 7 to August 28, 1976.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the second of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the singer is on the <a href="/blog/artists/elton-john/">Elton John</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1977: "I Just Want to Be Your Everything" by Andy Gibb</h3>
        <p>Barry Gibb wrote "I Just Want to Be Your Everything."</p>
        <p>It was recorded in October 1976 at Criteria Studios in Miami.</p>
        <p>RSO released it in April 1977 as the first single from Andy Gibb's debut album Flowing Rivers.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it spent four weeks at number one in two separate runs.</p>
        <p>The Emotions' "Best of My Love" interrupted it.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows July 30, August 6 and August 13, 1977, and then September 17.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the third of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Three Times a Lady" by Commodores</h3>
        <p>Lionel Richie wrote "Three Times a Lady."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the idea came from a toast his father gave at his parents' 37th anniversary party.</p>
        <p>Richie wrote it as a waltz for his wife, Brenda, and imagined Frank Sinatra singing it.</p>
        <p>Producer James Carmichael insisted it go on the Commodores' album Natural High instead.</p>
        <p>Motown released it on June 9, 1978.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, August 12 and August 19, 1978.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 10 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Good Times" by Chic</h3>
        <p>Bernard Edwards and Nile Rodgers wrote and produced "Good Times."</p>
        <p>They recorded it in 1978 at Power Station in New York City.</p>
        <p>Atlantic released it on June 4, 1979, as the first single from the album Risqué.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says its bassline was recreated in the Sugarhill Gang's "Rapper's Delight."</p>
        <p>Rodgers and Edwards threatened legal action.</p>
        <p>That ended in a settlement and co-writer credits on that record.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of August 18, 1979.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says The Knack's "My Sharona" replaced it the following week.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains August 19 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 20 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

